C ============================================================
C Annotated template for PySeshat's Tokovinin ORBIT-format
C visual/RV input file. Copy this file, replace every value
C below with your own system's, and parse it the same way
C Example 1 (examples/01_visual_rv_binary_fit.py) parses
C FIN379.inp:
C
C     from pyseshat.io.orbit_parser import parse_orbit_file
C     parsed = parse_orbit_file("your_orbit.inp")
C
C Lines starting with "C" (like this one) are comments --
C parse_orbit_file() skips them entirely. Blank lines are
C also skipped. Column parsing is done by
C src/pyseshat/io/orbit_parser.py; this template mirrors
C exactly what it recognizes (see that file's docstring for
C the authoritative reference).
C ============================================================

C --- Metadata (optional, informational only -- not used by
C     the fit itself) -----------------------------------------
Object:  Your System Name
RA:      12.3456
Dec:    -45.6789

C --- Starting orbital elements ------------------------------
C Tag   Value           Meaning
C P     period (years)
C T     epoch of periastron passage (decimal year)
C e     eccentricity (0 <= e < 1)
C a     angular semi-major axis (arcsec)
C W     Omega, longitude of ascending node (degrees)
C w     omega, argument of periastron (degrees)
C i     inclination (degrees)
C K1    primary RV semi-amplitude (km/s) -- omit if no RV data
C K2    secondary RV semi-amplitude (km/s) -- omit if no RV data
C V0    systemic velocity (km/s) -- omit if no RV data
C
C Prefix any tag with "*" (e.g. "*i") to hold it FIXED at the
C given value instead of letting the optimizer vary it --
C parse_orbit_file() reads this into elements.fit_flags
C automatically.

P        10.0000
T        2015.000
e        0.300
a        0.200
W        45.0
w        90.0
i        60.0
K1       12.00
K2       15.00
V0        5.00

C --- Visual astrometric observations ------------------------
C A line is read as a visual observation whenever it contains
C the literal token "I1" somewhere on the line. The first four
C whitespace-separated tokens are read as:
C
C     epoch  theta(deg)  rho(arcsec)  sigma(arcsec)
C
C epoch may be a decimal year (<=3000) or JD-2400000 (>3000);
C parse_orbit_file() normalizes either automatically, even
C mixed within the same file. Extra trailing columns (quality
C flag, reference code, etc.) are allowed and ignored -- see
C FIN379.inp in validation/benchmark_systems/ for a real
C example with several such extra columns.

  2016.500   50.00   0.180   0.003  I1
  2018.200  140.00   0.190   0.003  I1
  2020.100  230.00   0.175   0.003  I1

C --- Radial-velocity observations ----------------------------
C A line is read as an RV observation whenever it contains the
C literal token "Va" (primary) or "Vb" (secondary). The first
C two tokens are read as:
C
C     epoch  RV(km/s)
C
C the sigma column position is not fixed by tag position (only
C "Va"/"Vb" is matched, wherever it appears), so keep the
C convention epoch, RV, sigma, ... Va/Vb consistent with your
C other rows.

  2016.500   14.20   0.30  Va
  2016.500   -2.10   0.30  Vb
  2018.200    9.80   0.30  Va
  2018.200    1.90   0.30  Vb
